{"data":[{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12746","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12746","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12746","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tony","familyName":"Lindeberg","name":"Lindeberg, Tony","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zahra","familyName":"Babaiee","name":"Babaiee, Zahra","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peyman M.","familyName":"Kiasari","name":"Kiasari, Peyman M.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Modelling and analysis of the 8 filters from the \"master key filters hypothesis\" for depthwise-separable deep networks in relation to idealized receptive fields based on scale-space theory"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T07:04:45Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:28:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-23T14:44:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-21T01:10:57Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1007/s10851-026-01290-0","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This paper presents the results of analysing and modelling a set of 8 ``master key filters'', which have been extracted by applying a clustering approach to the receptive fields learned in depthwise-separable deep networks based on the ConvNeXt architecture.\n For this purpose, we first compute spatial spread measures in terms of weighted mean values and weighted variances of the absolute values of the learned filters, which support the working hypotheses that: (i) the learned filters can be modelled by separable filtering operations over the spatial domain, and that (ii) the spatial offsets of the those learned filters that are non-centered are rather close to half a grid unit. Then, we model the clustered ``master key filters'' in terms of difference operators applied to a spatial smoothing operation in terms of the discrete analogue of the Gaussian kernel, and demonstrate that the resulting idealized models of the receptive fields show good qualitative similarity to the learned filters.\n This modelling is performed in two different ways: (i) using possibly different values of the scale parameters in the coordinate directions for each filter, and (ii) using the same value of the scale parameter in both coordinate directions. Then, we perform the actual model fitting by either (i) requiring spatial spread measures in terms of spatial variances of the absolute values of the receptive fields to be equal, or (ii) minimizing the discrete $l_1$- or $l_2$-norms between the idealized receptive field models and the learned filters.\n Complementary experimental results then demonstrate the idealized models of receptive fields have good predictive properties for replacing the learned filters by idealized filters in depthwise-separable deep networks, thus showing that the learned filters in depthwise-separable deep networks can be well approximated by discrete scale-space filters."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"25 pages, 5 figures, 11 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12746","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-09-17T01:47:32Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:47:33Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-21T03:13:30Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12300","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12300","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12300","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Adnaan","familyName":"Nauthoo","name":"Nauthoo, Adnaan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peter","familyName":"Dunsby","name":"Dunsby, Peter","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Álvaro","familyName":"de la Cruz-Dombriz","name":"de la Cruz-Dombriz, Álvaro","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Past and future isotropization in $f(R)$ dark energy models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"General Relativity and Quantum Cosmology (gr-qc)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T18:00:00Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:02:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-10-06T08:38:00Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-07T00:52:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1103/r2sq-44mh","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We present a dynamical system analysis for the homogeneous and anisotropic Bianchi I cosmology in the context of Hu-Sawicki $f(R)$ gravity with parameters $\\{n,C_{1}\\}=\\{1,1\\}$. Deriving the corresponding four-dimensional dynamical system from the field equations allows us to find the fixed points of the system in both the vacuum and matter cases alongside their stability. This approach also provides analytical expressions for both the cosmic expansion and shear of each fixed point. More importantly, it allows us to address one of the least satisfying aspects of the $Λ$CDM picture: the apparent fine-tuning of the initial state. We show that, for the chosen parameters, Hu-Sawicki gravity allows for isotropization both in the past and in the future for both vacuum and matter, demonstrating that Hu-Sawicki gravity may alleviate the anisotropy-related initial-condition problem for a subsequent inflationary phase. For the matter case, the analysis was subdivided into the dust and radiation cases, and we show that both cases exhibit past and future isotropization. The obtained results also revealed interesting aspects of the phase space such as the existence and phenomenology of lines and two-dimensional sheets of fixed points."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Version published in PRD, minor changes, conclusions unchanged"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12300","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-09-17T01:37:17Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:37:18Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-07T03:15:38Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12635","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12635","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12635","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sheng","familyName":"Shen","name":"Shen, Sheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rémi","familyName":"Munos","name":"Munos, Rémi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hongyuan","familyName":"Zhan","name":"Zhan, Hongyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuandong","familyName":"Tian","name":"Tian, Yuandong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Positional Encoding via Token-Aware Phase Attention"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T03:53:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T16:56:27Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-25T21:11:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-29T00:10:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-10T22:28:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-05-12T01:30:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-09-06T17:50:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-09-09T01:09:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Zero v1.0 Universal","rightsIdentifier":"cc0-1.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We prove under practical assumptions that Rotary Positional Embedding (RoPE) introduces an intrinsic distance-dependent bias in attention scores that limits RoPE's ability to model long-context. RoPE extension methods may alleviate this issue, but they typically require post-hoc adjustments after pretraining, such as rescaling or hyperparameters retuning. This paper introduces Token-Aware Phase Attention (TAPA), a new positional encoding method that incorporates a learnable phase function into the attention mechanism. TAPA preserves token interactions over long range, extends to longer contexts with direct and light continual pretraining, extrapolates to unseen lengths, and attains substantially lower perplexity and stronger retrieval performance in the long-context regime than RoPE-style baselines."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"28 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12635","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":4,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:44:59Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:44:59Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-09T05:53:55Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12703","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12703","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12703","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gyungmin","familyName":"Cho","name":"Cho, Gyungmin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dohun","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Dohun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Sample-optimal single-copy quantum state tomography with shallow-depth measurements"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Quantum Physics (quant-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T05:54:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:25:18Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-07-26T10:28:41Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-07-28T00:50:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-17T18:31:38Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-08-19T00:04:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Quantum state tomography (QST) is a central task in quantum information, and its efficiency is commonly characterized by sample complexity. Although collective measurements on multiple copies achieve optimal performance, they are difficult to implement on near-term devices, motivating the study of single-copy approaches. Here, we introduce an ancilla-free single-copy QST protocol based on logarithmic-depth local circuits on an $n$-qubit system. For rank-$r$ states in dimension $d=2^n$, our protocol achieves trace-norm error $ε$ using $\\mathcal{O}(dr^2\\log d/ε^2)$ copies, matching the single-copy lower bound up to a logarithmic factor. For full-rank mixed states, it removes this logarithmic overhead and achieves the optimal scaling $\\mathcal{O}(d^3/ε^2)$, with nearly optimal classical runtime for explicit matrix output. These results show that sample-optimal QST can be realized using experimentally accessible shallow-depth measurements."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12703","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:46:32Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:46:33Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-19T02:23:45Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.13236","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.13236","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.13236","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fitsum Sileshi","familyName":"Beyene","name":"Beyene, Fitsum Sileshi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Christopher L.","familyName":"Dancy","name":"Dancy, Christopher L.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Layout-Aware OCR for Black Digital Archives with Unsupervised Evaluation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Digital Libraries (cs.DL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T16:43:34Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-05T01:05:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1109/istas65609.2025.11269615","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Despite their cultural and historical significance, Black digital archives continue to be a structurally underrepresented area in AI research and infrastructure. This is especially evident in efforts to digitize historical Black newspapers, where inconsistent typography, visual degradation, and limited annotated layout data hinder accurate transcription, despite the availability of various systems that claim to handle optical character recognition (OCR) well. In this short paper, we present a layout-aware OCR pipeline tailored for Black newspaper archives and introduce an unsupervised evaluation framework suited to low-resource archival contexts. Our approach integrates synthetic layout generation, model pretraining on augmented data, and a fusion of state-of-the-art You Only Look Once (YOLO) detectors. We used three annotation-free evaluation metrics, the Semantic Coherence Score (SCS), Region Entropy (RE), and Textual Redundancy Score (TRS), which quantify linguistic fluency, informational diversity, and redundancy across OCR regions. Our evaluation on a 400-page dataset from ten Black newspaper titles demonstrates that layout-aware OCR improves structural diversity and reduces redundancy compared to full-page baselines, with modest trade-offs in coherence. Our results highlight the importance of respecting cultural layout logic in AI-driven document understanding and lay the foundation for future community-driven and ethically grounded archival AI systems."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"IEEE-ISTAS conference"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.13236","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-09-17T01:59:04Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:59:04Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-05T02:29:32Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12502","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12502","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12502","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jonathan","familyName":"Pelletier","name":"Pelletier, Jonathan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Baptiste","familyName":"Royer","name":"Royer, Baptiste","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Enlarging the GKP stabilizer group for enhanced noise protection"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Quantum Physics (quant-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T23:09:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:11:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-24T13:49:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-25T00:50:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-30T15:32:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-07-28T01:40:22Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.22331/q-2026-07-08-2156","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Encoding a qubit in a larger Hilbert space of an oscillator is an efficient way to protect its quantum information against decoherence. Promising examples of such bosonic encodings are the Gottesman-Kitaev-Preskill (GKP) codes. In this work, we investigate how redefining the stabilizer group of the GKP codes to include all operations with trivial action on the code space can contribute to the search for an optimal implementation of a logical circuit when it is affected by noise. We find the generators of the Gaussian stabilizer group, allowing us to search for different physical implementations of a Clifford operation. We then propose an algorithm that finds the optimal implementation of a given logical Clifford circuit on GKP codes, such that the state is less affected by loss errors during the computation. Finally, we demonstrate numerically, with logical randomized benchmarking, that such a compiler can increase the lifetime of square-GKP qubits while running Clifford circuits, compared to a random walk compiler."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12502","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":4,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-09-17T01:41:53Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:41:53Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-07-28T03:47:55Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.13100","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.13100","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.13100","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rakesh K.","familyName":"Dora","name":"Dora, Rakesh K.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ajit C.","familyName":"Balram","name":"Balram, Ajit C.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Dispersion of collective modes in spinful fractional quantum Hall states on the sphere"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Strongly Correlated Electrons (cond-mat.str-el)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T13:57:39Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:48:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-04-25T14:00:24Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-28T00:32:31Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-16T05:02:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-05-19T00:30:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1103/17p5-jkym","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Collective modes capture the dynamical aspects of fractional quantum Hall (FQH) fluids. Depending on the active degrees of freedom, different types of collective modes can arise in a FQH state. In this work, we consider spinful FQH states in the lowest Landau level (LLL) along the Jain sequence of fillings $ν{=}n/(2n{\\pm}1)$ and compute the Coulomb dispersion of their spin-flip and spin-conserving collective modes in the spherical geometry. We use the LLL-projected density-wave and composite fermion (CF) exciton states as trial wave functions for these modes. To evaluate the dispersion of density-wave states, we derive the commutation algebra of spinful LLL-projected density operators on the sphere, which enables us to extract the gap of the density-wave excitations from the numerically computed density-density correlation function, i.e., the static structure factor, of the FQH ground state. We find that the CF excitons provide an accurate description of the collective modes at all wavelengths, while the density-wave states fail to do so. Specifically, the spin-flip density wave reliably captures the spin-flip collective mode only for the Laughlin and Halperin states, and that too only in the long-wavelength limit. Interestingly, for spin-singlet primary Jain states, the spin-conserving density mode is inaccurate even in the long-wavelength regime. We show that this discrepancy stems from the presence of an additional high-energy spin-conserving parton mode, similar to that found in fully polarized secondary Jain states at $ν{=}n/(4n{\\pm}1)$. We propose an ansatz for this parton mode and compute its Coulomb dispersion in the singlet state at $ν{=}2/5$. The predicted parton mode can be observed in circularly polarized inelastic light scattering experiments."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"33 pages, 15 figures, published version"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.13100","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:55:55Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:55:56Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-19T04:47:23Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12539","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12539","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12539","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Robin","familyName":"Vujanic","name":"Vujanic, Robin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Thomas","familyName":"Rueckstiess","name":"Rueckstiess, Thomas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"LEAF: Knowledge Distillation of Text Embedding Models with Teacher-Aligned Representations"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T00:41:05Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:13:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-04-20T02:29:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-21T01:34:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We present LEAF (\"Lightweight Embedding Alignment Framework\"), a knowledge distillation framework for text embedding models. A key distinguishing feature is that our distilled leaf models are aligned to their teacher. In the context of information retrieval, this allows for flexible asymmetric architectures where documents are encoded with the larger teacher model, while queries can be served with the smaller leaf models. We also show that leaf models automatically inherit MRL and robustness to output quantization whenever these properties are present in the teacher model, without explicitly training for them. To demonstrate the capability of our framework we publish leaf-ir, a 23M parameters information retrieval oriented text embedding model trained using LEAF, which sets a new state-of-the-art (SOTA) on BEIR, ranking #1 on the public leaderboard for this benchmark and for models of its size. When run in asymmetric mode, its retrieval performance is further increased. Our scheme is however not restricted to the information retrieval setting, and we demonstrate its wider applicability by synthesizing the multi-task leaf-mt model. This also sets a new SOTA, ranking #1 on the public MTEB v2 (English) leaderboard for its size. LEAF is applicable to black-box models and in contrast to other embedding model training frameworks, it does not require judgments nor hard negatives, and training can be conducted using small batch sizes. Thus, dataset and training infrastructure requirements for our framework are modest. We make our models publicly available under a permissive Apache 2.0 license."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"17 pages, 12 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12539","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:42:45Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:42:46Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-21T04:48:53Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12639","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12639","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12639","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jun","familyName":"Ye","name":"Ye, Jun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jun Yong","familyName":"Khoo","name":"Khoo, Jun Yong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"EmuPlat: A Framework-Agnostic Platform for Quantum Hardware Emulation with Validated Transpiler-to-Pulse Pipeline"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Quantum Physics (quant-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T03:56:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-04-01T00:44:39Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1145/3773656.3773674","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We present EmuPlat, a framework-agnostic quantum hardware emulation platform that addresses the interoperability gap between high-level quantum programming frameworks and hardware-specific pulse control systems. Unlike existing solutions that operate within isolated software stacks, EmuPlat provides a unified infrastructure enabling seamless integration across diverse quantum computing ecosystems, including CUDA-Q, Qiskit, and Qibolab. The platform implements a complete transpiler-compiler pipeline that systematically transforms abstract quantum circuits through four validated stages: (1) recursive gate decomposition to a minimal native set $\\mathcal{G}_{\\text{native}} = \\{I, Z, RZ(θ), \\text{GPI2}(ϕ), CZ, M\\}$, (2) virtual Z optimization implementing phase tracking without physical pulses, (3) connectivity-aware routing with automated SWAP insertion, and (4) deterministic pulse compilation respecting hardware timing constraints. Our modular architecture, based on clean architecture principles with a novel adapter pattern, supports extensible integration of multiple quantum dynamics simulation engines while maintaining consistent interfaces. We demonstrate EmuPlat's capabilities through comprehensive benchmarks on superconducting transmon architectures: Bell state preparation achieves 99.958\\% fidelity with hardware-calibrated noise models, while 4-qubit Quantum Fourier Transform implementations successfully demonstrate scalable circuit execution. The platform's production-ready implementation, validated through end-to-end testing with TransformationValidator, establishes EmuPlat as essential infrastructure for accelerating hybrid quantum-classical algorithm development and hardware-software co-design."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"8 pages, 4 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12639","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-09-17T01:45:04Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:45:05Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-01T02:30:29Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.13012","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.13012","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.13012","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kazuyuki","familyName":"Tsuda","name":"Tsuda, Kazuyuki","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Perturbation theory of the compressible Navier-Stokes equations and its application"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Analysis of PDEs (math.AP)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"35Q30, 35B20, 76N06, 35L05","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T12:33:55Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:44:17Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-20T11:33:00Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-23T01:30:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In this article, a perturbation theory of the compressible Navier-Stokes equations in $\\mathbb{R}^n$ $(n \\geq 3)$ is studied to investigate decay estimate of solutions around a non-constant state. As a concrete problem, stability is considered for a perturbation system from a stationary solution $u_ω$ belonging to the weak $L^n$ space. Decay rates of the perturbation including $L^\\infty$ norm are obtained which coincide with those of the heat kernel except a bit loss. The proof is based on deriving suitable resolvent estimates with perturbation terms in the low frequency part having a parabolic spectral curve. Our method can be applicable to dispersive hyperbolic systems like wave equations with strong damping. Indeed, a parabolic type decay rate of a solution is obtained for a damped wave equation including variable coefficients which satisfy spatial decay conditions."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Revised a proof of Proposition 4.15"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.13012","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:53:47Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:53:48Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-23T02:44:49Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12253","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12253","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12253","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Riyaadh","familyName":"Gani","name":"Gani, Riyaadh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Physics-Informed Neural Networks vs. Physics Models for Non-Invasive Glucose Monitoring: A Comparative Study Under Noise-Stressed Synthetic Conditions"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Image and Video Processing (eess.IV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-12T12:18:00Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:01:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-20T14:36:11Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-23T01:37:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Non-invasive glucose monitoring outside controlled settings is dominated by low signal-to-noise ratio (SNR): hardware drift, environmental variation, and physiology suppress the glucose signature in NIR signals. We present a noise-stressed NIR simulator that injects 12-bit ADC quantisation, LED drift, photodiode dark noise, temperature/humidity variation, contact-pressure noise, Fitzpatrick I-VI melanin, and glucose variability to create a low-correlation regime (rho_glucose-NIR = 0.21). Using this platform, we benchmark six methods: Enhanced Beer-Lambert (physics-engineered ridge regression), Original PINN, Optimised PINN, RTE-inspired PINN, Selective RTE PINN, and a shallow DNN. The physics-engineered Beer Lambert model achieves the lowest error (13.6 mg/dL RMSE) with only 56 parameters and 0.01 ms inference, outperforming deeper PINNs and the SDNN baseline under low-SNR conditions. The study reframes the task as noise suppression under weak signal and shows that carefully engineered physics features can outperform higher-capacity models in this regime."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12253","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:36:13Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:36:13Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-23T02:44:46Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.13171","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.13171","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.13171","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Luca","familyName":"Lunati","name":"Lunati, Luca","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Enrico","familyName":"Pierobon","name":"Pierobon, Enrico","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Uli","familyName":"Weber","name":"Weber, Uli","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tim","familyName":"Wagner","name":"Wagner, Tim","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tabea","familyName":"Pfuhl","name":"Pfuhl, Tabea","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marco","familyName":"Durante","name":"Durante, Marco","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Christoph","familyName":"Schuy","name":"Schuy, Christoph","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Hybrid Active-Passive Galactic Cosmic Ray Simulator: in-silico design and optimization"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Space Physics (physics.space-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T15:22:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:53:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-21T13:02:19Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-24T01:36:20Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-14T15:16:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-01-15T01:47:01Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-02-04T12:42:19Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-02-05T01:47:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"High-energy heavy-ion particle accelerators have long served as proxies for the harsh space radiation environment, enabling both fundamental life-science research and applied testing of flight hardware. Traditionally, monoenergetic high-energy heavy-ion beams have been employed for practicality, providing valuable datasets that underpin radiation risk and predictive computational models. However, such beams cannot fully reproduce the mixed-field nature of space radiation, motivating the development of realistic analogs for improved risk assessment and countermeasure evaluation in preparation for future deep-space missions to Moon or Mars. Spearheaded by developments at the NASA Space Radiation Laboratory, the GSI Helmholtzzentrum fuer Schwerionenforschung, supported by the European Space Agency (ESA), has established advanced space radiation simulation capabilities in Europe. Here, we present the design, optimization, and in-silico benchmarking of GSI's hybrid active-passive Galactic Cosmic Ray (GCR) simulator, together with a computationally optimized phase-space particle source for Geant4, which is available to external users for their own simulation studies and experimental planning."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.13171","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:57:34Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:57:35Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-05T03:20:20Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.13158","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.13158","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.13158","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Enrico","familyName":"Pierobon","name":"Pierobon, Enrico","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Luca","familyName":"Lunati","name":"Lunati, Luca","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tim","familyName":"Wagner","name":"Wagner, Tim","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marco","familyName":"Durante","name":"Durante, Marco","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Christoph","familyName":"Schuy","name":"Schuy, Christoph","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Hybrid Active-Passive Galactic Cosmic Ray Simulator: experimental implementation and microdosimetric characterization"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Space Physics (physics.space-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Instrumentation and Methods for Astrophysics (astro-ph.IM)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T15:12:16Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:52:43Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-20T13:33:24Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-21T01:40:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-14T15:10:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-01-15T01:46:30Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-02-04T09:35:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-02-05T01:38:20Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Space radiation is one of the major obstacles to space exploration. If not mitigated, radiation can interact both with biological and electronic systems, inducing damage and posing significant risk to space missions. Countermeasures can only be studied effectively with ground-based accelerators that act as a proxy for space radiation. Following an in-silico design and optimization process we have developed a galactic cosmic ray (GCR) simulator using a hybrid active-passive methodology. In this approach, the primary beam energy is actively switched and the beam interacts with specifically designed passive modulators. In this paper, we present the implementation of such a GCR simulator and its experimental microdosimetric characterization. Measuring the GCR field is of paramount importance, both before providing it to the user as a validated radiation field and for achieving the best possible radiation description. The issue is addressed in this paper by using a tissue equivalent proportional counter to measure radiation quality and by comparing experimental measurements with Monte Carlo simulations. In conclusion, we will demonstrate the GCR simulator's capability to reproduce a GCR field."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.13158","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:57:15Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:57:15Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-05T03:20:19Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12335","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12335","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12335","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"L. W. K.","familyName":"Goh","name":"Goh, L. W. K.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"A. N.","familyName":"Taylor","name":"Taylor, A. N.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Phantom Crossing with Quintom Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cosmology and Nongalactic Astrophysics (astro-ph.CO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T18:04:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-26T14:34:25Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-12-15T14:34:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-16T02:37:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1093/mnras/staf1927","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We develop a two-scalar field quintom model, which utilises both a quintessence-like and a phantom-like scalar field, enabling a smooth and stable transition across the $w=-1$ phantom divide as hinted by recent measurements of Baryonic Acoustic Oscillations (BAO) by the Dark Energy Spectroscopic Instrument (DESI) Data Release 2. We explore a range of initial conditions and potential configurations that facilitate such a phantom-to-quintessence-like crossing, and find that this can be naturally realised with hill-top or cliff-face potentials bound from above. We study how varying these conditions affects the dynamics of the system, calculate the background observables and compare them with DESI, CMB, and Type Ia supernova data, identifying a viable parameter space for our model. In particular, we find that a potential featuring a hyperbolic tangent form can successfully reproduce the desired phantom crossing, although such models can suffer from fine-tuning effects. Finally, we discuss prospects for distinguishing such models with upcoming state-of-the-art cosmological observations."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Submitted to MNRAS, comments welcome"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12335","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-09-17T01:38:06Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:38:06Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-12-16T04:49:21Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12934","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12934","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12934","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jeremias","familyName":"Ferrao","name":"Ferrao, Jeremias","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Matthijs","familyName":"van der Lende","name":"van der Lende, Matthijs","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ilija","familyName":"Lichkovski","name":"Lichkovski, Ilija","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Clement","familyName":"Neo","name":"Neo, Clement","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"The Anatomy of Alignment: Decomposing Preference Optimization by Steering Sparse Features"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T10:32:40Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:39:30Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-25T20:31:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-29T00:08:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-01T01:57:33Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-12-02T02:04:16Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Prevailing alignment methods induce opaque parameter changes, obscuring what models truly learn. To address this, we introduce Feature Steering with Reinforcement Learning (FSRL), a framework that trains a lightweight adapter to steer model behavior by modulating interpretable sparse features. First, we theoretically demonstrate that this mechanism is expressive enough to approximate the behavioral shifts of post-training processes. We then apply FSRL to preference optimization and perform a causal analysis of the learned policy. Our analysis reveals a crucial insight: the model learns to reward stylistic presentation as a proxy for quality, disproportionately relying on features related to style and formatting over those tied to alignment concepts like honesty. By effectively optimizing the preference objective, FSRL serves as a transparent proxy for observing the alignment process. Overall, FSRL offers an interpretable control interface and a practical way to diagnose how preference optimization pressures manifest at the feature level."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Spotlight at NeurIPS 2025 Mechanistic Interpretability Workshop"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12934","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:51:59Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:52:00Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-12-02T04:46:36Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12496","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12496","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12496","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ali","familyName":"Torabi","name":"Torabi, Ali","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sanjog","familyName":"Gaihre","name":"Gaihre, Sanjog","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"MD Mahbubur","familyName":"Rahman","name":"Rahman, MD Mahbubur","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yaqoob","familyName":"Majeed","name":"Majeed, Yaqoob","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Localized Region Guidance for Class Activation Mapping in WSSS"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T22:41:44Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:11:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-20T00:24:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-21T01:11:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Weakly Supervised Semantic Segmentation (WSSS) addresses the challenge of training segmentation models using only image-level annotations. Existing WSSS methods struggle with precise object boundary localization and focus only on the most discriminative regions. To address these challenges, we propose IG-CAM (Instance-Guided Class Activation Mapping), a novel approach that leverages instance-level cues and influence functions to generate high-quality, boundary-aware localization maps. Our method introduces three key innovations: (1) Instance-Guided Refinement using object proposals to guide CAM generation, ensuring complete object coverage; (2) Influence Function Integration that captures the relationship between training samples and model predictions; and (3) Multi-Scale Boundary Enhancement with progressive refinement strategies. IG-CAM achieves state-of-the-art performance on PASCAL VOC 2012 with 82.3% mIoU before post-processing, improving to 86.6% after CRF refinement, significantly outperforming previous WSSS methods. Extensive ablation studies validate each component's contribution, establishing IG-CAM as a new benchmark for weakly supervised semantic segmentation."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12496","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:41:45Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:41:46Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-11-21T03:02:33Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12929","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12929","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12929","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Maria-Catalina","familyName":"Isfan","name":"Isfan, Maria-Catalina","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Laurentiu-Ioan","familyName":"Caramete","name":"Caramete, Laurentiu-Ioan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ana","familyName":"Caramete","name":"Caramete, Ana","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daniel","familyName":"Tonoiu","name":"Tonoiu, Daniel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alexandru","familyName":"Nicolin-Żaczek","name":"Nicolin-Żaczek, Alexandru","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Quantum Computing Tools for Fast Detection of Gravitational Waves in the Context of LISA Space Mission"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"General Relativity and Quantum Cosmology (gr-qc)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Cosmology and Nongalactic Astrophysics (astro-ph.CO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Instrumentation and Methods for Astrophysics (astro-ph.IM)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Data Analysis, Statistics and Probability (physics.data-an)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T10:26:54Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:38:58Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-24T07:56:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-25T00:32:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-16T10:15:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10-17T00:42:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The field of gravitational wave (GW) detection is progressing rapidly, with several next-generation observatories on the horizon, including LISA. GW data is challenging to analyze due to highly variable signals shaped by source properties and the presence of complex noise. These factors emphasize the need for robust, advanced analysis tools. In this context, we have initiated the development of a low-latency GW detection pipeline based on quantum neural networks (QNNs). Previously, we demonstrated that QNNs can recognize GWs simulated using post-Newtonian approximations in the Newtonian limit. We then extended this work using data from the LISA Consortium, training QNNs to distinguish between noisy GW signals and pure noise. Currently, we are evaluating performance on the Sangria LISA Data Challenge dataset and comparing it against classical methods. Our results show that QNNs can reliably distinguish GW signals embedded in noise, achieving classification accuracies above 98\\%. Notably, our QNN identified 5 out of 6 mergers in the Sangria blind dataset. The remaining merger, characterized by the lowest amplitude, highlights an area for future improvement in model sensitivity. This can potentially be addressed using additional mock training datasets, which we are preparing, and by testing different QNN architectures and ansatzes."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Submitted to Classical and Quantum Gravity v2: corrected typo in axis label in Fig.4 v3: corrected typo in author name"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12929","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:51:52Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:51:53Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-17T04:12:14Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12995","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12995","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12995","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yue","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Yue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinan","familyName":"He","name":"He, Xinan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kaiqing","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Kaiqing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bing","familyName":"Fan","name":"Fan, Bing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Feng","familyName":"Ding","name":"Ding, Feng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jinhua","familyName":"Zeng","name":"Zeng, Jinhua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bin","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Bin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Brought a Gun to a Knife Fight: Modern VFM Baselines Outgun Specialized Detectors on In-the-Wild AI Image Detection"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T12:06:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:43:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-18T02:30:49Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-19T00:15:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-15T03:04:56Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10-16T00:21:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"While specialized detectors for AI-generated images excel on curated benchmarks, they fail catastrophically in real-world scenarios, as evidenced by their critically high false-negative rates on `in-the-wild' benchmarks. Instead of crafting another specialized `knife' for this problem, we bring a `gun' to the fight: a simple linear classifier on a modern Vision Foundation Model (VFM). Trained on identical data, this baseline decisively `outguns' bespoke detectors, boosting in-the-wild accuracy by a striking margin of over 20\\%.\n Our analysis pinpoints the source of the VFM's `firepower': First, by probing text-image similarities, we find that recent VLMs (e.g., Perception Encoder, Meta CLIP2) have learned to align synthetic images with forgery-related concepts (e.g., `AI-generated'), unlike previous versions. Second, we speculate that this is due to data exposure, as both this alignment and overall accuracy plummet on a novel dataset scraped after the VFM's pre-training cut-off date, ensuring it was unseen during pre-training. Our findings yield two critical conclusions: 1) For the real-world `gunfight' of AI-generated image detection, the raw `firepower' of an updated VFM is far more effective than the `craftsmanship' of a static detector. 2) True generalization evaluation requires test data to be independent of the model's entire training history, including pre-training."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12995","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:53:23Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:53:24Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-16T02:25:09Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12662","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12662","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12662","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zequn","familyName":"Xie","name":"Xie, Zequn","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chuxin","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Chuxin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sihang","familyName":"Cai","name":"Cai, Sihang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yeqiang","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yeqiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shulei","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Shulei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tao","familyName":"Jin","name":"Jin, Tao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Chat-Driven Text Generation and Interaction for Person Retrieval"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"I.2.7; I.4.9","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T04:40:24Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:22:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-30T02:20:05Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-01T00:24:57Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Text-based person search (TBPS) enables the retrieval of person images from large-scale databases using natural language descriptions, offering critical value in surveillance applications. However, a major challenge lies in the labor-intensive process of obtaining high-quality textual annotations, which limits scalability and practical deployment. To address this, we introduce two complementary modules: Multi-Turn Text Generation (MTG) and Multi-Turn Text Interaction (MTI). MTG generates rich pseudo-labels through simulated dialogues with MLLMs, producing fine-grained and diverse visual descriptions without manual supervision. MTI refines user queries at inference time through dynamic, dialogue-based reasoning, enabling the system to interpret and resolve vague, incomplete, or ambiguous descriptions - characteristics often seen in real-world search scenarios. Together, MTG and MTI form a unified and annotation-free framework that significantly improves retrieval accuracy, robustness, and usability. Extensive evaluations demonstrate that our method achieves competitive or superior results while eliminating the need for manual captions, paving the way for scalable and practical deployment of TBPS systems."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to EMNLP 2025 Main Conference. 13 pages, 3 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12662","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:45:36Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:45:37Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-01T02:59:58Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12408","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12408","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12408","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yaqing","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Yaqing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vikram","familyName":"Mohanty","name":"Mohanty, Vikram","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nikolas","familyName":"Martelaro","name":"Martelaro, Nikolas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aniket","familyName":"Kittur","name":"Kittur, Aniket","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yan-Ying","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Yan-Ying","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Matthew K.","familyName":"Hong","name":"Hong, Matthew K.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Scaffolding Flexible Ideation Workflows with AI in Creative Problem-Solving"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T19:55:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:06:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-19T01:46:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-22T00:15:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-25T14:53:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09-26T00:55:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Divergent thinking in the ideation stage of creative problem-solving demands that individuals explore a broad design space. Yet this exploration rarely follows a neat, linear sequence; problem-solvers constantly shift among searching, creating, and evaluating ideas. Existing interfaces either impose rigid, step-by-step workflows or permit unguided free-form exploration. To strike a balance between flexibility and guidance for augmenting people's efficiency and creativity, we introduce a human-AI collaborative workflow that supports a fluid ideation process. The system surfaces three opt-in aids: (1) high-level schemas to uncover alternative ideas, (2) risk analysis with mitigation suggestions, and (3) steering system-generated suggestions. Users can invoke these supports at any moment, allowing seamless back-and-forth movement among design actions to maintain creative momentum."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted as a poster to ACM Collective Intelligence 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12408","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:39:44Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:39:45Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-26T02:17:04Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12845","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12845","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12845","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xin","familyName":"Fang","name":"Fang, Xin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guirui","familyName":"Zhong","name":"Zhong, Guirui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qing","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Qing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fan","familyName":"Chu","name":"Chu, Fan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lei","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Lei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mengui","familyName":"Qian","name":"Qian, Mengui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mingqi","familyName":"Cai","name":"Cai, Mingqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiangzhao","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Jiangzhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jianqing","familyName":"Gao","name":"Gao, Jianqing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jun","familyName":"Du","name":"Du, Jun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Improving Anomalous Sound Detection with Attribute-aware Representation from Domain-adaptive Pre-training"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Sound (cs.SD)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T09:05:41Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:34:31Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-19T06:17:41Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-22T00:26:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Anomalous Sound Detection (ASD) is often formulated as a machine attribute classification task, a strategy necessitated by the common scenario where only normal data is available for training. However, the exhaustive collection of machine attribute labels is laborious and impractical. To address the challenge of missing attribute labels, this paper proposes an agglomerative hierarchical clustering method for the assignment of pseudo-attribute labels using representations derived from a domain-adaptive pre-trained model, which are expected to capture machine attribute characteristics. We then apply model adaptation to this pre-trained model through supervised fine-tuning for machine attribute classification, resulting in a new state-of-the-art performance. Evaluation on the Detection and Classification of Acoustic Scenes and Events (DCASE) 2025 Challenge dataset demonstrates that our proposed approach yields significant performance gains, ultimately outperforming our previous top-ranking system in the challenge."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Copyright 2026 IEEE. Personal use of this material is permitted. Permission from IEEE must be obtained for all other uses, in any current or future media, including reprinting/republishing this material for advertising or promotional purposes, creating new collective works, for resale or redistribution to servers or lists, or reuse of any copyrighted component of this work in other works"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12845","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:49:55Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:49:55Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-22T02:46:09Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.13107","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.13107","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.13107","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kohou","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Kohou","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huan","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Huan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiang","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Xiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zezhou","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Zezhou","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ping","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Ping","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhaoxiang","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Zhaoxiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shiguo","familyName":"Lian","name":"Lian, Shiguo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Hierarchical Deep Fusion Framework for Multi-dimensional Facial Forgery Detection -- The 2024 Global Deepfake Image Detection Challenge"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T14:06:54Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T14:25:24Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The proliferation of sophisticated deepfake technology poses significant challenges to digital security and authenticity. Detecting these forgeries, especially across a wide spectrum of manipulation techniques, requires robust and generalized models. This paper introduces the Hierarchical Deep Fusion Framework (HDFF), an ensemble-based deep learning architecture designed for high-performance facial forgery detection. Our framework integrates four diverse pre-trained sub-models, Swin-MLP, CoAtNet, EfficientNetV2, and DaViT, which are meticulously fine-tuned through a multi-stage process on the MultiFFDI dataset. By concatenating the feature representations from these specialized models and training a final classifier layer, HDFF effectively leverages their collective strengths. This approach achieved a final score of 0.96852 on the competition's private leaderboard, securing the 20th position out of 184 teams, demonstrating the efficacy of hierarchical fusion for complex image classification tasks."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"The 2024 Global Deepfake Image Detection Challenge Top20 Reward, 5 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.13107","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:56:04Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:56:05Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-19T02:08:44Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12681","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12681","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12681","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yutaka","familyName":"Yamamoto","name":"Yamamoto, Yutaka","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kaoru","familyName":"Yamamoto","name":"Yamamoto, Kaoru","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Nonlinear Sampled-data Systems--A Lifting Framework"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Systems and Control (eess.SY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"93C10, 93C57, 93C62","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T05:08:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T14:26:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1016/j.ifacol.2023.10.840","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This short note gives a new framework for dealing with nonlinear sampled-data systems. We introduce a new idea of lifting, which is well known for linear systems, but not successfully generalized to nonlinear systems. This paper introduces a new lifting technique for nonlinear, time-invariant systems, which are different from the linear counterpart as developed in [Bamieh et al. 1991, Yamamoto 1994], etc. The main difficulty is that the direct feedthrough term effective in the linear case cannot be generalized to the nonlinear case. Instead, we will further lift the state trajectory, and obtain an equivalent time-invariant discrete-time system with function-space input and output spaces. The basic framework, as well as the closed-loop equation with a discrete-time controller, is given. As an application of this framework, we give a representation for the Koopman operator derived from the given original nonlinear system."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12681","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-09-17T01:46:02Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:46:02Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-19T02:08:36Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12444","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12444","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12444","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weiting","familyName":"Feng","name":"Feng, Weiting","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kyle L.","familyName":"Walker","name":"Walker, Kyle L.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yunjie","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Yunjie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Francesco","familyName":"Giorgio-Serchi","name":"Giorgio-Serchi, Francesco","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Computing forward statics from tendon-length in flexible-joint hyper-redundant manipulators"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Systems and Control (eess.SY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T20:50:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T13:24:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Hyper-redundant tendon-driven manipulators offer greater flexibility and compliance over traditional manipulators. A common way of controlling such manipulators relies on adjusting tendon lengths, which is an accessible control parameter. This approach works well when the kinematic configuration is representative of the real operational conditions. However, when dealing with manipulators of larger size subject to gravity, it becomes necessary to solve a static force problem, using tendon force as the input and employing a mapping from the configuration space to retrieve tendon length. Alternatively, measurements of the manipulator posture can be used to iteratively adjust tendon lengths to achieve a desired posture. Hence, either tension measurement or state estimation of the manipulator are required, both of which are not always accurately available. Here, we propose a solution by reconciling cables tension and length as the input for the solution of the system forward statics. We develop a screw-based formulation for a tendon-driven, multi-segment, hyper-redundant manipulator with elastic joints and introduce a forward statics iterative solution method that equivalently makes use of either tendon length or tension as the input. This strategy is experimentally validated using a traditional tension input first, subsequently showing the efficacy of the method when exclusively tendon lengths are used. The results confirm the possibility to perform open-loop control in static conditions using a kinematic input only, thus bypassing some of the practical problems with tension measurement and state estimation of hyper-redundant systems."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"To be presented at IROS 2025, Hangzhou, China"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12444","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:40:34Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:40:35Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-19T02:08:28Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.13178","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.13178","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.13178","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Claudio","familyName":"Battiloro","name":"Battiloro, Claudio","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Andrea","familyName":"Cavallo","name":"Cavallo, Andrea","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Elvin","familyName":"Isufi","name":"Isufi, Elvin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"CoVariance Filters and Neural Networks over Hilbert Spaces"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Signal Processing (eess.SP)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T15:32:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:54:04Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T14:12:40Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-18T00:44:43Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"CoVariance Neural Networks (VNNs) perform graph convolutions on the empirical covariance matrix of signals defined over finite-dimensional Hilbert spaces, motivated by robustness and transferability properties. Yet, little is known about how these arguments extend to infinite-dimensional Hilbert spaces. In this work, we take a first step by introducing a novel convolutional learning framework for signals defined over infinite-dimensional Hilbert spaces, centered on the (empirical) covariance operator. We constructively define Hilbert coVariance Filters (HVFs) and design Hilbert coVariance Networks (HVNs) as stacks of HVF filterbanks with nonlinear activations. We propose a principled discretization procedure, and we prove that empirical HVFs can recover the Functional PCA (FPCA) of the filtered signals. We then describe the versatility of our framework with examples ranging from multivariate real-valued functions to reproducing kernel Hilbert spaces. Finally, we validate HVNs on both synthetic and real-world time-series classification tasks, showing robust performance compared to MLP and FPCA-based classifiers."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"6 pages, 3 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.13178","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:57:43Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:57:44Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-18T17:48:48Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}}],"meta":{"total":66,"totalPages":3,"page":1},"links":{"self":"https://api.datacite.org/dois?client-id=arxiv.content\u0026query=registered%3A%5B2025-09-16T16%3A00%3A00Z+TO+2025-09-17T15%3A59%3A59.999Z%5D+AND+%28titles.title%3A%28kernel+OR+kernels+OR+accelerator+OR+accelerators+OR+quantization+OR+quantisation+OR+compiler+OR+compilers+OR+collective+OR+collectives+OR+pretraining+OR+pre-training+OR+post-training+OR+VLA+OR+VLM+OR+VLMs+OR+MLLM+OR+MLLMs+OR+RAG+OR+retrieval-augmented+OR+mixture-of-experts+OR+state-space+OR+KV-cache%29+OR+descriptions.description%3A%28kernel+OR+kernels+OR+accelerator+OR+accelerators+OR+quantization+OR+quantisation+OR+compiler+OR+compilers+OR+collective+OR+collectives+OR+pretraining+OR+pre-training+OR+post-training+OR+VLA+OR+VLM+OR+VLMs+OR+MLLM+OR+MLLMs+OR+RAG+OR+retrieval-augmented+OR+mixture-of-experts+OR+state-space+OR+KV-cache%29%29+AND+NOT+%28registered%3A%5B2025-09-16T16%3A00%3A00Z+TO+2025-09-17T15%3A59%3A59.999Z%5D+AND+%28%22language+model%22+OR+%22language+models%22+OR+LLM+OR+LLMs+OR+transformer+OR+transformers+OR+agent+OR+agents+OR+diffusion+OR+multimodal+OR+GPU+OR+MoE+OR+%22state+space%22+OR+%22wor